From d61aa27f727a44b2c8c06bca826caa6d874f6a36 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 21:35:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] automation replay: distrust a wall reset only in a
 retired process, not on code skew alone

PR #2625 made `trusted_resets_at` drop a build failure's `failure_resets_at` when this process ran stale
code, judged by `host_loops_retired()` OR `detect_code_skew()`. A gateway whose checkout moved but that is
not retiring still runs the replay itself, on the same stale code: a genuine SubscriptionQuotaUnavailable
replayed after the short delay, hit the same wall, failed and gave up, where before #2625 it waited for the
wall's reset and delivered. `notice_guarantee._retry_at` spent close-out attempts the same way.

Five whys: the replay gave up early -> it was due after REPLAY_MIN_DELAY_S, not at the reset -> skew alone
made `trusted_resets_at` return None -> #2625 treated "older code than the checkout" as "a successor runs
the replay" -> only a retired process (host loops released, being replaced) actually hands the replay to a
successor on newer code.

Fix: `stale_code_reason` (only used by `trusted_resets_at`) now returns `host_loops_retired()` and nothing
else. A skewed always-on backend that stays behind is still covered: the skew watchdog retires its host
loops once the skew persists (test_an_always_on_backend_behind_the_checkout_gives_the_lease_up...).

Proof: tests/hermes_cli/test_superseded_host_loops.py
- test_a_skewed_but_not_retired_process_still_waits_for_the_reset: RED on main (trusted_resets_at -> None),
  GREEN here (replay due and close-out retry_at at the reset).
- test_a_wall_failure_in_a_retired_process_replays_after_the_short_delay[skewed=False|True]: retired keeps
  the short delay.
All 13 test files importing automation_replay/notice_guarantee: 225 passed. scripts/check --base origin/main ok.

Card t_01a1d6b19bf73072 (review nit on PR #2625, review 5897920822). Reverse: revert this commit.
